# The demonstration (roadmap l4.md L11, D19; os-server.md P12), as `make
# run` shows it: init starts the clock and the counter, daemons, which put
# the time and how fast the counter counts - how much of the CPU nothing
# else wants - on the status line, while the shell runs commands. In a
# TEST_DEMO build init starts them as it does outside the tests, and the OS
# server puts their status on the log too, where the runner sets it apart
# ("status, NAME: TEXT", after the line each fell in). ps lists them, init's
# orphans; the clock, at the shell's priority, ticks on while a command
# spins, until ^C; the counter, below every process, does not count then.
# (MAME's AT counts some 22,000 a second: its first report, after 65,536
# counts, takes three seconds.)
program: shell
define: TEST_DEMO
emulators: bochs mame
install: C /BIN/PS z-ps.rx
install: C /BIN/SPIN z-spin.rx
keys: {wait 250}{wait 100}ps -e\nspin\n{wait 150}{now ctrl-c}echo done\n
timeout: 120
match: contains
---
/a$ ps -e
status, clock:
status, counter: idle
   11     1    11    11 -
   13     1    13    13 -    R 39 counter
/a$ spin
Spinning; ^C ends it.
^C
status, clock:
status, clock:
/a$ echo done
done
